@page "/assistant-panels"

<CatHead Title="Assistant panels">
    Where an agent's work sits beside the app rather than over it: a conversation docked at the edge of
    the frame, and the file, query or document it generated kept open next to the conversation. Both are
    the app's markup in classes the library already has &mdash; a <a href="/drawer">drawer</a>, a
    <a href="/chat"><code>.chat-pane</code></a>, a <a href="/grid"><code>.sedna-split</code></a> &mdash;
    with one variant to dock the drawer and one small family for the generated thing.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.AssistantPanels.DockedAssistant" Title="Assistant beside the page" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--flush">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; open it with the sparkle in the header.</strong>
        <code>.drawer--dock</code> on a <code>&lt;dialog class="drawer" data-modeless&gt;</code> that is a
        direct child of <code>.layout</code>, after <code>.content</code>. From 900px wide it is a column
        of the frame: open, it takes <code>--drawer-width</code> at the inline end and
        <strong>the content narrows instead of being covered</strong>; closed, it takes no room. Below
        900px it is the ordinary drawer over the page's edge, and the page keeps its width. It sits at the
        inline end, so it mirrors under <code>dir="rtl"</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        It opens and closes like any <a href="/drawer">modeless drawer</a>:
        <code>sednaUi.modal.show(id)</code> or <code>ISednaUi.ShowModalAsync(id)</code>, and its own close
        button. The toggle carries <code>aria-controls</code> naming the dialog and an
        <code>aria-expanded</code>, which Sedna.UI.js keeps in step with the dialog however it closes.
        A <code>.chat-pane</code> straight after the <code>.drawer-header</code> takes the height that is
        left, with the composer at the foot.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.AssistantPanels.ArtifactBeside" Title="File beside the conversation" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>.artifact</code> is what an agent generated, kept where the reader can read it while they
        talk about it. <code>.artifact-head</code> holds <code>.artifact-head-text</code> &mdash; the
        <code>.artifact-title</code> and a line under it saying what it is &mdash; and
        <code>.artifact-actions</code>: the <a href="/agent-chat"><code>.chat-versions</code></a> switcher,
        copy with <code>data-copy-target</code>, download as a link with <code>download</code>, open in a
        new tab, and a close button last when there is one. Each action is a
        <code>.btn--ghost .btn--icon .btn--sm</code> with its own name.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>.artifact-body</code> pads prose; <code>.artifact-body--flush</code> gives the edge to a
        <code>.code-block--flush</code> or a table. Here it is the aside of a
        <code>.sedna-split--lg .sedna-split--stack-md</code>, so it sits beside the conversation and moves
        under it when the split is narrow.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.AssistantPanels.ArtifactDrawer" Title="Document in a drawer" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; open it.</strong> A <code>.drawer</code> holding an <code>.artifact</code> and
        nothing else: the artifact gives up its own frame for the drawer's and fills it, its head standing
        in for the <code>.drawer-header</code>. With <code>data-modeless</code> the conversation stays usable
        beside it; in a <code>.layout</code>, <code>.drawer--dock</code> makes it a column of the frame like
        the assistant above.
    </p>
</CatExample>
